# Build output produced by `npm run build:dom-bench` (esbuild bundles `dom-bench/entry.ts`
# into an ES module the page can import over http). It is an artifact, like everything under
# `dist/` and `public/bench/`, and it goes stale the moment the packages it re-exports are
# rebuilt — which is exactly how a benchmark ends up measuring last month's code.
dom-bench/bundle.js

# Recorded axis tables, written by `run-one-axis.js <axis> --record=<label>` and read by
# `npm run gate -- <axis> <before> <after>`. They are measurements of ONE machine in ONE
# thermal state, so they are meaningless anywhere but the session that produced them —
# committing them would invite exactly the comparison the gate exists to forbid.
.gate/
#
# ── AND THE OTHER HALF, WHICH IS COMMITTED ON PURPOSE ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `measurements/` is NOT ignored. It holds the recordings that PUBLISHED figures cite — the ones
# `scripts/check-doc-numbers.mjs` traces every number in `docs-site/docs/**` and `README.md` back to.
#
# The split is by PURPOSE, not by age, and it was made after checking rather than assuming: with
# every recording in `.gate/`, `npm run check:numbers` on a fresh clone failed with TEN problems —
# verified by moving the directory aside and running it. CI runs `release:check`, which runs that
# gate. A published number whose evidence sits on one laptop is an unsourced number for every other
# reader, and a gate that can only pass on that laptop is not a gate.
#
# What stays here: A/B pairs. `importnode-before`/`importnode-after`, `pre7arg`/`post7arg`,
# `viastage0a`/`viastage0b` — two measurements of ONE machine in ONE session, meaningless to anyone
# who did not run them back to back, and committing them would invite exactly the cross-machine
# comparison the reason above forbids.
#
# What goes to `measurements/`: anything a document quotes. If you publish a figure, its recording
# is part of the claim and belongs in the repository beside it.
